---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Related from "../components/Related.astro";
import { PINNED_KEY, IDENTITY, STORED_ON_DEVICE, storedCounts, countWords } from "../lib/site.ts";

/**
 * AN ORDINARY PRIVACY POLICY. Deliberately short, deliberately unremarkable.
 *
 * THIS PAGE HAS NOW BEEN WRONG IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, which is the reason it is written the way
 * it is. The first version was an argument: "no analytics, no third-party scripts, no
 * off-origin requests", plus three paragraphs on a Cloudflare beacon. Then the site ran Google
 * Analytics 4 and those sentences became false, so the page was rewritten as a compliance
 * document. GA4 was then removed and the GA-shaped half of that rewrite became false in turn,
 * so it was rewritten again — into a paragraph saying GA "is gone and is not coming back".
 * On 27 August 2026 the owner asked for it back, and that sentence became the fourth false
 * thing this page has said about analytics.
 *
 * IT IS REPLACED IN THE OPEN RATHER THAN DELETED. The published text now says what the page
 * used to promise and that the promise was withdrawn, because a privacy page that revises its
 * commitments silently is worth less than one that never made them — a reader who read the old
 * sentence and came back has no other way to find out.
 *
 * The lesson is not "be vague". It is that this page must describe WHAT RUNS, checked, on the
 * day it is edited. So there are no gates left in it. There is no GA_ENABLED flag deciding
 * which of two paragraphs a reader sees, because that flag is what let the page describe a
 * configuration instead of a fact. Every claim below was measured against the live site.
 *
 * AND IT HAS NOW BEEN WRONG IN A THIRD WAY, which is why the Analytics section reads as it
 * does. It said "None run on this site", and that was true of the site's own code and true of
 * what a browser executed — but the sentence next to it, that Cloudflare's injected beacon
 * "does not run", was paired in the CSP comment with "the tag itself is being turned off at
 * the dashboard". That never happened. For eight days the page carried a script it could not
 * execute, the account recorded zero pageloads, and a comment in src/middleware.ts described
 * an intention in the present tense. The beacon is now permitted on purpose, and every claim
 * about what it does below is either quoted from Cloudflare with its URL or measured — the
 * cookie and query-string claims are measurements of the beacon's own source, which anybody
 * can repeat with curl and grep.
 *
 * THE ONE SUBTLE THING, and the reason the Cloudflare section is longer than a hosting note
 * has any right to be: Cloudflare injects a measurement beacon into HTML responses, and it
 * decides whether to do so from the request's `accept` HEADER. Isolated on the live site on 19
 * August, four ways: an HTML accept gets the tag and 359 extra bytes; a wildcard accept does
 * not; the user-agent changes nothing in either direction. The first pass at this note said it
 * keyed off the user-agent, which was a guess dressed as a measurement — the difference was
 * sitting in a header that had not been varied. scripts/verify-live.mjs had the correct
 * explanation written at the top of it the whole time.
 *
 * It matters because a check that fetches this page without a browser's accept header sees a
 * clean document and reports one. verify-live sends a real browser's and therefore does see
 * it; the ad-hoc curl that produced the page-weight baseline did not, and reported zero. A
 * page describing what a reader receives has to be written from a request shaped like a
 * reader's.
 *
 * 31 OCTOBER 2026: GOOGLE ANALYTICS OUT, POSTHOG IN, on the operator's decision. The GA4
 * section is replaced by a PostHog one, with one sentence saying GA was removed and when — the
 * same rule as above, a reader who read the old section has no other way to find out. The two
 * _ga cookies left the table with the tag. What the PostHog section says about its own
 * collection (EU storage, the daily-salted IP hash, retention, legal basis) is the operator's
 * configuration of the project; what it says about this site's requests — no cookie, no
 * storage, origin and path only — was measured on the live site the day it shipped.
 */
/* WHAT THIS SITE PUTS ON YOUR MACHINE comes from STORED_ON_DEVICE in src/lib/site.ts, which
   /about reads as well — see the note there. The summary below counts the same rows the table
   renders, so the two cannot disagree; before 16 September they did, on this page and on /about. */
const { cookies: cookieCount, storage: storageCount } = storedCounts();

const title = "Privacy";
const description =
  `What Coinliqui collects: two analytics counters that store nothing on your device, ` +
  `${countWords(cookieCount, "cookie", "cookies")} and ` +
  `${countWords(storageCount, "local-storage key", "local-storage keys")} of the site's own, each named and ` +
  "explained here — and no payment details, no account and no personal information.";
/* THE DATE IS THE ONE ON THE MOST RECENT CHANGE TO WHAT THIS PAGE DESCRIBES, not the date of
   the last edit to the file. 31 October 2026: Google Analytics 4 was removed and PostHog
   replaced it. (Before that, 27 August 2026: the Cloudflare beacon was permitted and GA4 was
   restored, on the same day.) */
const updated = "31 October 2026";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p>What this site collects, and what it does not. Last updated {updated}.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="section">What we do not take</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    No payment details — nothing on this site is for sale and there is no checkout. No personal
    information: no name, email address, password, wallet connection or exchange API key is
    asked for anywhere. There are no accounts today, so there is nothing to register for and
    nothing to sign into. If that changes, this page changes with it.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">Analytics</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    <strong>Two counters run in your browser here: Cloudflare Web Analytics and PostHog.</strong> A third count, on the server and without any script, is described <a class="link" href="#arrival-count">after them</a>.
    Cloudflare's has been permitted since 27&nbsp;August&nbsp;2026; PostHog's since
    31&nbsp;October&nbsp;2026, when it replaced Google Analytics. They answer different
    questions, which is the only reason both are here: Cloudflare's says whether anybody loaded
    a page, cheaply and without a cookie, and stops there. PostHog's says which page a reader
    went to next and what they clicked — the number that decides which of the fifty contract
    pages is worth writing more about. Neither one sets a cookie or stores anything in your
    browser. Each is described below, separately.
  </p>
  <h3 class="subsection">Cloudflare Web Analytics</h3>
  <p class="prose">
    A script Cloudflare injects into this site's pages at its edge. It was blocked by this
    site's Content-Security-Policy until 27&nbsp;August&nbsp;2026 and is now allowed by it.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    What it sends: the page's origin and <strong>path</strong>, the referring page if your
    browser supplies one, and page-load timings taken from the browser's own Performance API.
    Cloudflare states that it "does not collect or use your visitors' personal data" and that
    it "does not track individual end users across our customers' Internet properties" —
    <a class="link" href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/" rel="nofollow noopener">their documentation</a>.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    Those are their words. These are measurements, made on 27&nbsp;August&nbsp;2026 and
    repeatable by anyone:
  </p>
  <div class="panel">
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th>Claim</th><th>How it was checked, and how you can</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>It sets no cookie and no browser storage</td>
          <td class="reason">The beacon's source contains no <code>document.cookie</code>, no <code>localStorage</code>, no <code>sessionStorage</code> and no <code>indexedDB</code> — fetch the script named in this page's markup and search it. Loaded on a live site running the same beacon, the page ended with zero cookies and zero storage keys.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>It does not send the query string</td>
          <td class="reason">The beacon reads <code>location.pathname</code> and <code>location.origin</code> and rebuilds the URL from those two. It never reads <code>location.href</code> or <code>location.search</code>; neither string appears in the file at all. This is the reason the calculators below are unaffected by it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>It does not fingerprint</td>
          <td class="reason">No canvas, no audio context, no device enumeration in the source. What it reads is the Performance API, which reports how long the page took to load and nothing about you.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The data goes to this domain, not a third one</td>
          <td class="reason">The measurement is posted to <code>/cdn-cgi/rum</code> on this origin, so this counter sends nothing to a third party. The site's <code>connect-src</code> is no longer <code>'self'</code> alone: it also names PostHog's two EU hosts, because the second counter does post off-origin. That difference is set out below.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="section-note" style="margin-top:10px">
    Two hosts are permitted to serve a script here: <code>static.cloudflareinsights.com</code>
    and <code>eu-assets.i.posthog.com</code>. There is no third — you can read the whole
    policy in this page's response headers.
  </p>
  <h3 class="subsection">PostHog</h3>
  <p class="prose">
    <strong>Google Analytics was removed from this site on 31&nbsp;October&nbsp;2026</strong>, and
    PostHog took its place.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    PostHog is product analytics made by PostHog&nbsp;Inc., and what it collects here is stored
    in the EU, in Frankfurt. It <strong>sets no cookies and stores nothing in your
    browser</strong> — no local storage and no session storage. What it records: the pages you
    view, clicks on links and buttons, the page that referred you, your browser and device
    type, your screen size, and how fast the page loaded.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    <strong>Your IP address is not stored.</strong> PostHog hashes it together with your
    browser's user agent and a salt that changes every day, uses the result only to count
    unique visitors for that day, and then discards it — so a visit today cannot be linked to a
    visit tomorrow. There are no session recordings. Nothing here identifies you, nothing is
    used for advertising, and nothing is sold. What is recorded is kept for up to one year. The
    legal basis is legitimate interest: knowing which pages are read is how this site decides
    what to write and what to fix.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    Two details are this site's own, and both are in <code>/analytics.js</code>, the file that
    starts it. Every address PostHog is given is cut to the origin and the path before it is
    sent, so figures in a calculator's URL never reach it. And clicks inside the
    <a class="link" href="/watchlist">watchlist</a>'s table — its pin buttons and its rows — are
    left out of click recording, so the coins you pin are not part of what it sends.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    The library comes from <code>eu-assets.i.posthog.com</code> and is fetched <strong>after
    the page has finished</strong> — on your first scroll, tap or key, when the browser goes
    idle, or three seconds in, whichever comes first — and what it records goes to
    <code>eu.i.posthog.com</code>. That is a speed measure: the library is about 108&nbsp;KB
    over the wire, more than most pages here. Blocking either host stops it completely, and
    nothing else on this site depends on them. There is no consent banner: nothing is stored on
    your device, and nothing PostHog records identifies you.
  </p>

  <h3 class="subsection" id="arrival-count">An arrival count on this site's own server</h3>
  <p class="prose">
    Since 28&nbsp;October&nbsp;2026 the server that answers each page also adds one to a daily
    tally when a browser opens a page because a person asked it to. It runs no script in your
    browser, sets no cookie and stores nothing about you: a row in the tally is a date, the page,
    the host of the site or the <code>utm_source</code> the link came from, whether the page was
    typed or bookmarked, linked from another site or reached from a page of this one, phone
    or desktop, and the two-letter country Cloudflare reports for the request. No IP address, no browser string and no identifier is written, so a row cannot be
    tied to a person. It exists because the counters above depend on JavaScript running in your
    browser, and in October the two then running — Cloudflare's, which samples and drops the
    referrer, and Google's — left a fall in readers unexplained.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">Cookies and local storage</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Kind</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Expires</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {STORED_ON_DEVICE.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td><code set:text={r.name} /></td>
            <td>{r.kind === "cookie" ? "Cookie" : "Local storage"}</td>
            <td set:html={r.purpose} />
            <td>{r.expires}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="section-note" style="margin-top:10px">
    Two entries, both this site's own, and neither holds an identifier. Neither counter adds
    anything to this list: the two Google Analytics cookies, <code>_ga</code> and
    <code>_ga_5Y4ZENWMQJ</code>, went with Google Analytics on 31&nbsp;October&nbsp;2026, and a
    browser that visited before then may still hold them until they expire or you clear them.
    Clearing your browser data removes everything here and the site behaves the same without it.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">The calculators</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    The <a href="/tools">calculators</a> compute in the page and store nothing. Their forms
    submit as <code>GET</code>, so the values you enter appear in the page's own URL — which is
    what makes a result shareable, and also means those numbers reach ordinary web-server
    request logs. Nothing links them to you, but if you would rather a real account balance did
    not appear in a log line, use a representative figure.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    Those request logs are the only place the values go. Both counters are given the origin and
    the path and nothing else — Cloudflare's rebuilds the URL from those two, and PostHog's
    loader cuts every address in an event down to them before it is sent — so a figure typed
    into a field named <code>capital</code> is not part of what either one sends. That is a
    measurement of the requests, not an assurance.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    <strong>It has not always been true, and this page said it had stopped when it had not.</strong>
    The Google tag sends the full URL of every page view — query string included — unless it is
    told otherwise, and it was not told otherwise. That was the case from the day the tag first
    went in, through its removal on 19&nbsp;August&nbsp;2026, and again from its restoration on
    27&nbsp;August; the paragraph here described it in the past tense throughout those last four
    days, saying the sending had ended when the tag came out. It had not: the tag came back.
    Measured on 31&nbsp;August&nbsp;2026, loading the position-size calculator with figures in
    its URL produced a request to Google carrying that whole URL, those figures included. On the
    same day the tag was configured with <code>page_location</code> set to the origin and the
    path — the rule the Cloudflare beacon already followed — and re-measured. Nothing about the
    request logs above changed; what changed is that Google no longer receives the query string.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">Hosting</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    This site runs on Cloudflare. Serving a page means Cloudflare handles the request itself —
    your IP address, the page asked for, the browser string, the response status — and keeps
    aggregate traffic and security metrics from it. That much is true of any web host and is not
    optional for a site that is served at all.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    The measurement script described above is Cloudflare's too, and it is <strong>injected by
    the host rather than written into this site's source</strong>: it appears in the HTML after
    this site's code has finished with the response, and it does so whenever a client asks for
    the page the way a browser asks for it. It was blocked here until 27&nbsp;August&nbsp;2026
    and is permitted now. Apart from the two counters described above, nothing off-origin can
    serve a script, receive a fetch, or load an image on any page here.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">Contact</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    Questions about any of this, including a request to delete data associated with your
    visits: <!--email_off--><a class="link" href={`mailto:${IDENTITY.contact}`}>{IDENTITY.contact}</a><!--email_on-->.
  </p>

  <Related items={[
    { href: "/about", label: "About", note: "Who operates this site and what it is for." },
    { href: "/data-sources", label: "Data sources", note: "Every endpoint behind every number here." },
    { href: "/corrections", label: "Corrections", note: "Including what this page once said wrongly, and when it was fixed." },
    { href: "/methodology", label: "Methodology", note: "How the numbers are produced." },
    { href: "/watchlist", label: "Watchlist", note: "The one feature that stores anything, and it stores it in your browser." },
  ]} />

  <style>
    /* THE ONLY PAGE ON THIS SITE WITH A THIRD HEADING LEVEL, and it earns it: "Analytics" now
       has two counters under it that collect different things and cost the reader different
       amounts, and flattening them to siblings of their own parent would tell a screen reader
       that PostHog is a peer of Analytics rather than a case of it.

       NOT `.sub`, which was the first attempt. src/pages/unlocks.astro already defines .sub as
       a small faint inline label inside table cells; reusing the name for a heading is the
       `.lede` defect this codebase has already paid for once — one class name meaning two
       things in two files, with whichever stylesheet loads last deciding. Scoped here rather
       than promoted into the layout because exactly one page needs it. */
    h3.subsection { font-size: var(--fs-sm); font-weight: 650; letter-spacing: -.005em; color: var(--text-dim); margin: var(--gap-block) 0 var(--gap-tight); }
    h2.section { margin-top: 32px; }
  </style>
</Base>
